abstract:
We prove that under the heat semigroup $(P_\tau)$ on the Boolean hypercube, any nonnegative function exhibits a uniform tail bound that is better than Markov's inequality. Specifically, for any $\tau > 0$, $n \geq 1$, $\eta > e^3$, and $f: \{-1,1\}^n \to \mathbb{R}_+$ with $\int f d\mu > 0$, we have
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{P}_{X \sim \mu}\left( P_\tau f(X) > \eta \int f d\mu \right) \leq c_\tau \frac{ (\log \log \eta)^{\frac32} }{\eta \sqrt{\log \eta}},
\end{align*}
where $\mu$ is the uniform measure on the Boolean hypercube $\{-1,1\}^n$ and $c_\tau$ is a constant that depends only on $\tau$. This result resolves Talagrand's convolution conjecture up to a dimension-free $(\log \log \eta)^{\frac32}$ factor. Our proof uses the reverse heat process on the Boolean hypercube, a coupling construction with carefully engineered perturbations of jump rates and a time-smoothed anti-concentration estimate.

We give a query-optimal algorithm for simulating a general $n$-qubit time-dependent Hamiltonian $H(t)$ on $[0,T]$, assuming that $H$ is Lipschitz continuous and $\|H(t)\|\leq\alpha$. In the standard $\mathrm{HAM\mbox{-}T}$ access model, the algorithm approximates the time-ordered propagator $U_H(T)$ to error $\varepsilon$ using $$ O\left(
\alpha T+\frac{\log(1/\varepsilon)}
{\log(e+\log(1/\varepsilon)/(\alpha T))}
\right) $$ $\mathrm{HAM\mbox{-}T}$ queries. This matches the known query lower bound for time-independent Hamiltonians, showing that time dependence incurs no asymptotic query overhead.
Our method first constructs a one-query transducer that, given an auxiliary state, implements an approximation to $U_H(T)$ and returns the state unchanged. A weighted combination of circuits that apply the transducer different numbers of times makes the error caused by omitting this state decay factorially, yielding the stated optimal precision dependence. For time-independent Hamiltonians, the same method also gives a query-optimal alternative to qubitization.

Recently, a uniform $\frac{7}{8}$ zero-free half-plane for finite-order Hecke $L$-functions over $\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-3})$ and its transfer to Dirichlet $L$-functions was given by OpenAI in [Ope26a]. In its proof, there are several parameters $b=\frac{1}{8}, \ell=\frac{1}{6}$, and $\kappa$. In this paper, via varying $b$, $\ell$, and $\kappa$, following the same strategy, we slightly improve the bound $\frac{7}{8}=0.875$ to \[
B_{\mathrm{new}}=\frac{18\xi_{\mathrm{new}}+7}{18\xi_{\mathrm{new}}+9}
=0.874957019420098946\ldots, \] where $\xi_{\mathrm{new}}$ is the unique root in $(0.38858,0.38859)$ of $1188\xi^3-2160\xi^2+171\xi+190=0$. Explicitly, with $\arccos\in[0,\pi]$, \[
\xi_{\mathrm{new}}=\frac{20}{33}+\frac{\sqrt{1391}}{33}
\cos\!\left(\frac13\arccos\frac{28470}{1391^{3/2}}
-\frac{2\pi}{3}\right). \] The limiting parameters are \[ \begin{gathered}
b_{\mathrm{new}}=\frac{8(3\xi_{\mathrm{new}}-1)(12\xi_{\mathrm{new}}^2-40\xi_{\mathrm{new}}+15)}
{9(2\xi_{\mathrm{new}}+1)(24\xi_{\mathrm{new}}^2-20\xi_{\mathrm{new}}+5)},
\ell_{\mathrm{new}}=\frac{5-6\xi_{\mathrm{new}}}{9+18\xi_{\mathrm{new}}},\qquad
\kappa_{\mathrm{new}}=\frac{18\xi_{\mathrm{new}}+5}{18\xi_{\mathrm{new}}+9}
=2B_{\mathrm{new}}-1. \end{gathered} \] We also prove its optimality within the explicitly specified exponent comparison system. This result has been formalized by Lean [Liu26].

For a rotationally symmetric Ricci flow $g(t)=ds^2+f(s,t)^2g_{\mathbb S^{n-1}}$, $n\ge3$, we study the scale-invariant curvature anisotropy $A=1-f_s^2+ff_{ss}$ introduced by Angenent--Knopf. At the critical power $p_*=(n-1)/(n-2)$, with $\Phi=sgn(A)|A|^{p_*/2}$, we prove the distributional identity \[
(\partial_t-\Delta)|A|^{p_*}
=-\frac4{n-1}
\left|\Phi_s+(n-1)\frac{f_s}{f}\Phi\right|^2. \] Integration against a conjugate heat kernel gives an anisotropic entropy formula. As an application, we obtain an entropy threshold for Type-I singularities on $\mathbb S^n$: if the entropy associated with a singular-time kernel is at most $1$ at an earlier regular time, every tangent flow at the corresponding singular point is a round shrinking sphere. This criterion follows from the $0/1$ quantization of the singular-time anisotropic density.
